WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.459 --> 00:00:05.160
Speaker 1: Today, we're going to be speaking
with Andy Norman, who studies how ideologies

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:07.210
can short circuit minds.

00:00:07.210 --> 00:00:14.099
He's the author of the recently released book
Mental Immunity Infectious Ideas Mind Parasites

00:00:14.099 --> 00:00:16.240
and the Search for a Better Way to Think.

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:17.930
Andy, it's so great to have you on today.

00:00:17.930 --> 00:00:19.349
Speaker 5: Thank you, David.

00:00:19.349 --> 00:00:20.349
It's a pleasure to be here.

00:00:20.349 --> 00:00:27.439
Speaker 1: You know, there's all these analogies
these days to infection and immunity and and

00:00:27.439 --> 00:00:29.699
inoculation and vaccination.

00:00:29.699 --> 00:00:36.011
And you talk about the concept of a mental
immune system and a cultural immune system.

00:00:36.011 --> 00:00:38.500
Can you tell us a little bit about those ideas?

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:43.510
Speaker 5: Yeah, so just as our bodies have
immune systems that protect us from infectious

00:00:43.510 --> 00:00:49.579
microbes, our minds have immune systems that
protect us from infectious ideas.

00:00:49.579 --> 00:00:54.159
These immune systems aren't talked about a
great deal in the sciences yet, but the sciences

00:00:54.159 --> 00:00:57.850
are waking up to the fact that that they exist.

00:00:57.850 --> 00:01:03.920
There's now a mountain of evidence that they
do exist and had to exist because the wrong

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:07.990
ideas could get our ancestors killed.

00:01:07.990 --> 00:01:13.070
And so we're learning to actually science
is actually learning to view minds as having

00:01:13.070 --> 00:01:19.970
filtering systems that are meant to fact,
to screen out bad ideas and let in good ones.

00:01:19.970 --> 00:01:25.950
And of course, they can malfunction in all
kinds of ways that lead to social dislocation

00:01:25.950 --> 00:01:29.530
and political turmoil, among other things.

00:01:29.530 --> 00:01:35.250
Speaker 1: So sometimes people will ask me
what is the best way to protect oneself from

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:39.049
fake news and disinformation, propaganda,
et cetera.

00:01:39.049 --> 00:01:46.920
And in my mind, the inoculation that I suggest
is a foundation of critical thinking and understanding

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:52.080
how we evaluate whether something we've been
told is something we should believe and understanding

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:55.320
how media works and and media literacy.

00:01:55.320 --> 00:02:00.630
Is that an example of such an immune system?
Speaker 5: Absolutely.

00:02:00.630 --> 00:02:07.190
So I think the concept that comes closest
to mental immunity is critical thinking.

00:02:07.190 --> 00:02:11.370
And and so if you want to get a good head
start on an understanding of mental immunity,

00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:13.330
start with critical thinking for sure.

00:02:13.330 --> 00:02:17.750
It turns out, though, that the concept of
critical thinking is a very crude instrument

00:02:17.750 --> 00:02:21.300
for helping to build mental immunity.

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:26.170
And and it turns out the critical thinking
skills can be weaponized for an ideology not

00:02:26.170 --> 00:02:29.760
just employed to create fair minded thinkers.

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:33.510
Speaker 1: Can you give an example of how
of how that can happen?

00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:34.690
Speaker 5: Well, sure.

00:02:34.690 --> 00:02:39.470
So I think universities have been cranking
out people who are trained to one or another

00:02:39.470 --> 00:02:43.610
degree in critical thinking for decades and
decades.

00:02:43.610 --> 00:02:50.590
And often what happens is that I meant that
a college graduate will use their ability

00:02:50.590 --> 00:02:58.060
to dissect arguments, to defeat arguments
they dislike, but not to employ them to clarify

00:02:58.060 --> 00:02:59.650
or modify their own thinking.

00:02:59.650 --> 00:03:03.920
Speaker 1: So is that sort of a confirmation
type bias use of a tool?

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:05.320
Speaker 5: Exactly.

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:13.090
So psychologists call confirmation bias the
tendency to basically let in information that

00:03:13.090 --> 00:03:21.160
fits your political or ideological preconceptions,
let those in uncritically, but then respond

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:26.570
critically or cynically to information that
that challenges your point of view.

00:03:26.570 --> 00:03:32.160
And the problem with that is that's a recipe
for close mindedness and tribal tribal thinking.

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:36.510
And we absolutely need to get past that if
we hope to actually become wiser versions

00:03:36.510 --> 00:03:41.060
of ourselves
Speaker 1: when it comes to specific rhetorical

00:03:41.060 --> 00:03:42.060
tactics.

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:47.910
I'd be curious to see what you found about
what is more effective versus what is less

00:03:47.910 --> 00:03:49.040
effective.

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:53.880
We've talked on the program about things like
using a Socratic method of questioning to

00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:59.540
get one, to reexamine their own beliefs and
come to the conclusion themselves that they

00:03:59.540 --> 00:04:00.540
may be mistaken.

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:05.290
This is sort of, in a sense, I think, an idea
from Dale Carnegie from the 30s or whenever

00:04:05.290 --> 00:04:07.920
he wrote How to Win Friends and Influence
People.

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:14.580
And the defensibility question, which is what
evidence, if it existed, would get you to

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:15.810
change your mind.

00:04:15.810 --> 00:04:17.979
And then using that like as a jumping off
point.

00:04:17.979 --> 00:04:21.079
I mean, are these are these tools maybe effective?

00:04:21.079 --> 00:04:22.970
Speaker 5: Absolutely.

00:04:22.970 --> 00:04:28.710
So in the book, I argue that the famous Socratic
method is basically a way of testing ideas

00:04:28.710 --> 00:04:33.199
with questions and seeing if they can withstand
the questioning that that's one of the most

00:04:33.199 --> 00:04:35.010
powerful minded documents ever invented.

00:04:35.010 --> 00:04:40.199
And in the book, I take that mind innoculate
and refine it into something I called the

00:04:40.199 --> 00:04:45.460
new Socratic method, which I argue can be
something like a mind vaccine and take our

00:04:45.460 --> 00:04:48.440
critical thinking to a whole new level.

00:04:48.440 --> 00:04:52.810
I also love the question, what would change
your mind?

00:04:52.810 --> 00:04:59.419
That that is a powerful question for both
strengthening and modulating.

00:04:59.419 --> 00:05:05.249
Mental immune systems, so just as the the
body's immune system can get out of kilter

00:05:05.249 --> 00:05:13.320
and attacked the body's own tissues or let
in covid right, let in the coronavirus, the

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:16.740
mind's immune system can get it confused and
attack the wrong things.

00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:23.599
So a conspiracy theorist is somebody who becomes
hyper cynical about traditional or mainstream

00:05:23.599 --> 00:05:28.759
sources of information and their own critical
mindset.

00:05:28.759 --> 00:05:30.150
It's almost like an autoimmune disorder.

00:05:30.150 --> 00:05:35.150
Speaker 1: I was going to say it's almost
like this cytokine storm we talk about with

00:05:35.150 --> 00:05:41.090
the pandemic where the immune system goes
crazy because they're, you know, for whatever

00:05:41.090 --> 00:05:44.889
reason, I'm not a doctor, but, you know, this
overactive defense mechanism.

00:05:44.889 --> 00:05:48.069
Speaker 5: That's exactly, exactly the right
analogy.

00:05:48.069 --> 00:05:51.800
So cytokines are actually antibodies.

00:05:51.800 --> 00:05:56.449
They're the things the mind body's immune
system produces to fight off what it perceives

00:05:56.449 --> 00:05:57.840
to be a threat.

00:05:57.840 --> 00:06:02.520
And sometimes the body's immune system overreacts
to something that's genuinely harmless.

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:08.590
So when you have an allergic reaction to pollen,
that's your body overreacting to something

00:06:08.590 --> 00:06:10.639
that really doesn't harm it.

00:06:10.639 --> 00:06:12.699
It's the overreaction that makes you miserable.

00:06:12.699 --> 00:06:13.699
Right.

00:06:13.699 --> 00:06:17.449
And our minds can make the exact same kind
of mistake.

00:06:17.449 --> 00:06:22.729
We can attack genuinely reliable information
because it comes from somebody on the other

00:06:22.729 --> 00:06:28.669
side of the political divide, say, and in
this way, our minds do more to protect our

00:06:28.669 --> 00:06:33.009
chosen identities than they do to actually
help us find the truth.

00:06:33.009 --> 00:06:39.389
You actually have to train your minds immune
system to care about truth and to care less

00:06:39.389 --> 00:06:42.580
about your ideological identity.

00:06:42.580 --> 00:06:44.270
And when you do that, you become wiser.

00:06:44.270 --> 00:06:47.629
Speaker 1: Yeah, the conspiracy theory thing
is interesting.

00:06:47.629 --> 00:06:53.530
We've interviewed experts on conspiracies
and conspiratorial thinking, and they're you

00:06:53.530 --> 00:06:54.530
know what?

00:06:54.530 --> 00:07:01.629
How can you distinguish between healthy skepticism
and what's on the other side, which is this

00:07:01.629 --> 00:07:08.789
never ending and unsatisfying bull cynicism,
which is often what is at the core of conspiratorial

00:07:08.789 --> 00:07:09.789
thinking?

00:07:09.789 --> 00:07:11.810
Speaker 5: Yeah, you're exactly right.

00:07:11.810 --> 00:07:18.809
I think it's long been a tenet of the critical
think of of critical thinking advocates that

00:07:18.809 --> 00:07:21.629
we think critically, we ask the right questions.

00:07:21.629 --> 00:07:26.370
But those other guys right there are insufficiently
critical.

00:07:26.370 --> 00:07:30.159
But it turns out you can be too critical for
your own good, just as it's possible to be

00:07:30.159 --> 00:07:33.969
under critical it's possible to be overcritical.

00:07:33.969 --> 00:07:40.020
And so the truly healthy mind is one that
strikes the right balance between suspicion

00:07:40.020 --> 00:07:41.020
and trust.

00:07:41.020 --> 00:07:48.589
Speaker 1: Yeah, that you know, the a lot
of this, as you sort of alluded to, is my

00:07:48.589 --> 00:07:53.880
family is huge mental health family, a lot
of people and psych psychiatrists, psychologists,

00:07:53.880 --> 00:08:00.509
etc. and there's this really common psychotherapy
question, which is what would it mean to you

00:08:00.509 --> 00:08:05.550
if what you believe isn't true, which is really
about one's identity kind of being tied up

00:08:05.550 --> 00:08:06.550
in their beliefs.

00:08:06.550 --> 00:08:13.020
And so that's probably to some degree at the
core of why we attach so much to the beliefs

00:08:13.020 --> 00:08:14.089
we come in with.

00:08:14.089 --> 00:08:16.930
And and those beliefs are resistant to change
that.

00:08:16.930 --> 00:08:18.400
Speaker 5: That's right.

00:08:18.400 --> 00:08:24.159
And it turns out that we're more attuned to
the usefulness of ideas than we are to their

00:08:24.159 --> 00:08:25.159
truth.

00:08:25.159 --> 00:08:29.729
So if an idea feels useful to us, a lot of
times will cling to it even after it's been

00:08:29.729 --> 00:08:30.729
debunked.

00:08:30.729 --> 00:08:31.819
Speaker 1: Right.

00:08:31.819 --> 00:08:38.450
Speaker 5: And but to genuinely deepen your
understanding of the world and to become to

00:08:38.450 --> 00:08:44.290
grow morally, you actually have to learn to
care as much or more about truth as you do

00:08:44.290 --> 00:08:47.970
about the usefulness of the ideas that fill
your health.

00:08:47.970 --> 00:08:52.230
Your head, I should say, by the way, that
genuinely wise people don't look only at what

00:08:52.230 --> 00:08:59.040
I call upstream evidence for truth and also
care about the downstream causal consequences

00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:01.300
of what we believe.

00:09:01.300 --> 00:09:02.300
Right.

00:09:02.300 --> 00:09:03.300
Both matter.

00:09:03.300 --> 00:09:08.510
Speaker 1: The you know, I'm thinking of the
idea that some hold people who are economically

00:09:08.510 --> 00:09:16.010
disadvantaged, that it's, you know, immigrants
from Mexico that are responsible for them,

00:09:16.010 --> 00:09:21.780
either not having a job or having lost a job
or whatever the case may be.

00:09:21.780 --> 00:09:27.570
And that seems to fit that idea that you mention
of there's a convenience to believing that

00:09:27.570 --> 00:09:34.470
that there's a usefulness to that, because
maybe it takes attention away from other economic

00:09:34.470 --> 00:09:39.060
factors that are truly responsible or from
whatever personal responsibility there may

00:09:39.060 --> 00:09:43.560
be behind one's economic circumstances, whatever,
whatever the truth is

00:09:43.560 --> 00:09:45.180
Speaker 5: that that's exactly right.

00:09:45.180 --> 00:09:52.520
So scapegoat scapegoating immigrants is has
long been a staple of demagogs.

00:09:52.520 --> 00:09:59.090
And what it does is it is that it very reliably
hijacks mental immune systems of.

00:09:59.090 --> 00:10:05.270
The demagogs supporters, so if you peddle
fear of immigrants, a lot of times the fear

00:10:05.270 --> 00:10:13.660
will trigger auto in cognitive auto immune
overreactions right there by duping your own

00:10:13.660 --> 00:10:16.220
followers and by hero by your followers.

00:10:16.220 --> 00:10:17.830
I mean a demagogs followers.

00:10:17.830 --> 00:10:20.250
One's following one's followers.

00:10:20.250 --> 00:10:21.250
Right.

00:10:21.250 --> 00:10:28.050
So so fear mongers actually are hijacking
